[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: spine_router_0_0
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: the routing control plane (FRR/bgpd) on spine_router_0_0. Mechanism: When FRR (bgpd) stops, BGP keepalives to all neighbors cease, so every session to that spine transitions to Active within seconds, exactly as monitoring reported from the super-spine and both leaves. Data-plane forwarding remains intact via the other spine, so host-to-host traffic still succeeds, but the fabric loses redundancy/ECMP.

Localization: spine_router_0_0. Direct evidence shows its FRR service is not running (systemd: “frr.service … Active: inactive (dead)” and vtysh: “bgpd is not running”). Corroborating evidence from neighbors: 
- super_spine_router_0 BGP summary shows neighbor 172.16.0.1 (AS 65100 – spine_router_0_0) in Active, while the other spine neighbor is Established and exchanging prefixes.
- leaf_router_0_0 and leaf_router_0_1 each show one spine neighbor (AS 65100) in Active and the other spine neighbor Established with prefixes.

Root cause: FRR on spine_router_0_0 has stopped (service inactive), causing all BGP sessions to that spine to drop simultaneously.

Corrective fix: Restart and enable FRR on spine_router_0_0 (systemctl restart frr; ensure it’s enabled). After restart, verify “show bgp summary” on all neighbors returns Established for the AS 65100 peer and that ECMP routes are restored. If it stops again, inspect logs (journalctl -u frr, /var/log/frr/*) and configuration for errors or crashes before declaring resolved.
=== END ===